-- c/build.shale: the C's builds and the checks that hold its tests honest,
-- in two tiers.
--
--   shale c/build.shale          the fast tier, the inner loop between edits:
--                                tcc runs every test file, each held to its
--                                `TEST(` count (test_spawn's quick tests
--                                alone: SLOW_TESTS wait out real time),
--                                then the discipline check and its
--                                self-test, the command under tcc, and each
--                                block's counts.
--   shale c/build.shale full=1   the full tier, before a merge: the fast tier,
--                                then GCC's -O2 for its warnings and ASan for
--                                the tests, and every block's full-only
--                                checks: the harness fixture, the compiler's
--                                refusals, and every planted fault.
--
-- **The builds.** `tcc` runs each test file (`tcc -run`). `release` is
-- -std=c11 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror for its warnings alone: each test file
-- (the unity build's translation units, so every module's code) is compiled
-- with `-c`, never linked or run, since -Warray-bounds and -Wrestrict fire
-- only with the optimizer on and `-fsyntax-only` skips them; c/shale.c alone
-- is linked at -O2, the binary the differ judges. `asan` is -O0 with
-- -fsanitize=address,undefined, and runs every test file.
--
-- Every check runs in exactly one tier, and the full tier runs both.
--
-- **The blocks.** Each part of the C has a block below, a function taking
-- `t`, the run: `t.full` says which tier runs, and `t.builds` lists the
-- builds to run under, `{"tcc"}` in the fast tier and `{"tcc", "release",
-- "asan"}` in the full. A block runs its fast checks always and its others
-- under `if t.full then`, and the list at the bottom calls each block in
-- turn. A new block is a function and one line in that list. `run_in(t,
-- phase, argv, opts)` runs a program and charges its time to `phase`, and
-- the table at the end prints each phase's programs, wall time and CPU
-- time, so a slow run says where its time went. `test_file(t, phase, build,
-- src, extra?)` takes one test file through a build: run and held to its
-- count, or for `release` compiled for its warnings. The C is a unity build:
-- a test file `#include`s the `.c` files it needs, so each test file is one
-- compile, under tcc or GCC alike.
--
-- Plants are one-time proofs that a test can see its fault, so they live in
-- the full tier alone: a change that adds or moves a test or a plant runs
-- `full=1` before it merges. A block's plants are a list it hands to
-- `run_plants(t, phase, plants, extra?)`, each planted in a copy of
-- c/foundation, c/script and c/host (`dir` names the folder), and each
-- naming in `aim` the tests that catch it: its test file runs those alone
-- (`TEST_ONLY`, test.h), so a plant costs its own tests, not the file's.
--
-- It reads each compiler's version first. A compiler that is missing fails the
-- run, loud, after everything it can run has run.
--
-- Run from the repository's root: shale c/build.shale [full=1]
-- budget: steps=40000000 programs=400 arena_bytes=268435456 changes=20000 change_bytes=1073741824

const FOUNDATION = "c/foundation"
const OUT = "c/out"
const WANT = "c-tests: the build's own test binary, made under c/out"

-- Each fault planted in a copy of the foundation, one at a time: `file`'s
-- `old` (there exactly once) becomes `new`, and the test file `caught_by`
-- must fail on it (`harness` is the fixture's run). A block's own plants
-- (`REGEX_PLANTS`, `PARSE_PLANTS`) name `dir = "script"` for c/script.
const PLANTS = {
  {name = "the budget ignores what is held",
   aim = "the_budget_refuses_past_its_limit_and_says_it_was_hit,a_chunk_near_the_limit_takes_only_what_is_left,the_arena_never_holds_more_than_its_limit_whatever_grows_in_it", file = "arena.h", caught_by = "test_arena",
   old = "if (total > arena_sat_sub(a->limit, a->held)) {", new = "if (total > a->limit) {"},
